#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172927} :activity/id #uuid "da0a118d-376a-5230-a1dc-46ce7816f56d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172927 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173169 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nThis theorem tells us that the line integral of a conservative vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla f$ is determined by the values of $f$ at the endpoints $A$ and $B$ of $C$ only. The integral does *not* depend on the path between $A$ and $B.$ \n\nIn particular, if $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, then we say that $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r$ is **path-independent.**" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab2cc7cb-1fa4-5e9c-92d4-551aeb945c53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5145 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "16937ae1-27e8-5337-99ff-6a5dd1b9e446" :step/math-academy-id 32376 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171860 :question/id #uuid "06e592bb-3ebe-57f1-a282-635599780593" :question/math-academy-id "e-8753"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72015667-33a9-53e2-a0c4-09d8ce916b54" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields That Are Independent of Path"} :step/id #uuid "cab8518e-c3d7-56ce-a74a-1da8815349ad" :step/math-academy-id 32601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172097 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171903 :question/id #uuid "a9f1b212-7d45-566a-95d3-fa2697ad8c3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5598fb5a-b749-56ec-8b86-49d73206b58c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447005}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path"} :step/id #uuid "a55b03af-55fa-5584-8ba8-abdc7dda9f46" :step/math-academy-id 35770 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171904 :question/id #uuid "d659bb69-1a8d-5fb2-837c-27e454877021" :question/math-academy-id "e-9549"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28bf8f3a-e97e-5ec1-bb18-3ebfa213aa46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415277}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Over an Arbitrary Path When the Potential Function Is Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "d161b477-2d2f-52f2-881b-e7e6f77d4ecc" :step/math-academy-id 35774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172932}}] :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172180}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424098} :activity/id #uuid "a2243f17-089c-5e52-a019-0035fd16e9e3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424098 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35348fe3-9c3a-5329-8e93-1436583e7d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257251} {:answer/id #uuid "75b868e6-319b-563d-831f-92bee29e5558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257815} {:answer/id #uuid "8988ff7c-7ded-5728-ab29-45c3901aa9d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257990} {:answer/id #uuid "b84a0627-05af-5fa3-8be1-e5e0905f69e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258394} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe19d67-ace6-5f6f-971e-e78991d0fb68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258825}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258825} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac9611f-eb21-5ce8-9755-c7ccbc9751f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5064d3f6-e78c-56b7-8d2f-396471e7bc8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125726" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-3,1)$ to $(1,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-3,1)$ and $B(1,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(1,-2)-f(-3,1) \\\\  & =(1)^{2}(-2)-(-3)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =-2-9 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "342f3d25-ce3a-5f3c-b57d-c4d0387248ff" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a818aab-75ac-5369-aa04-7b5968c78bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424091} {:answer/id #uuid "5b41497c-0b03-518e-b827-be965cbad632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424092} {:answer/id #uuid "80844163-7141-53d5-b539-4be5b4478368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424093} {:answer/id #uuid "aa971f18-d59c-541a-a8c1-bc17ca502230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424094} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ca885d-c4f1-58d6-b2f7-daed73d87732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424095} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf3ea97d-4f26-5777-a472-6a585002f0c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af7a572f-0636-5725-9441-ef6582928ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125729" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-4,1)$ and $B(0,-2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-2)-f(-4,1) \\\\  & =({0}^{2}-2(-2)^{3})-((-4)^{2}-2(1)^{3}) \\\\  & =16-16+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "64a5e538-5a25-5466-a330-6b9f2d52b686" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259851 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15deefdc-05ae-5086-a5f0-c1dc6044e9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256977} {:answer/id #uuid "2247900f-b21b-584e-a22b-427516ec3d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257062} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d5949-bfc8-5763-bd36-6a74f995a834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257566} {:answer/id #uuid "7187435a-9b2a-5138-bdc3-8e258afbb442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8bcb5c-084b-52f8-b5f5-1853ba91623b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258561}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} :answer-field/id #uuid "568ddf7f-5eb1-53a7-8971-663ff621802f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30e624bb-f7e4-561a-be9c-3d2d751b4f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-125735" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,1)$ to $(0,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2,1)$ and $B(0,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,3)-f(-2,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{0}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1}{(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =3-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9d21a388-bd37-58ca-99a7-53b6bd2958a7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424102}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046fdfb7-5908-5b1c-90e7-05dd1219d510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256819} {:answer/id #uuid "0494c2e3-4f93-51c9-97b4-abdfd2bbd422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256820} {:answer/id #uuid "0c87d320-18b0-5f77-bb7b-34ad58ca38b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc2089f-6561-53d8-b5ee-acdc74d9f43b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257182} {:answer/id #uuid "c02c18c1-a46b-54b2-8cf2-af2aecbb3d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258459}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256902} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f5a55be-ee74-53ab-9b52-5cbae09ff6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d312ac8-d0a5-5f87-aa22-804248c3f5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-125732" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-\\pi ,0)$ to $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-\\pi ,0)$ and $B(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(-\\pi ,0) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(1-1)-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf2721f1-961a-51a7-9951-c9a433cd01de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06828943-2517-5788-afbf-83f2f355cc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256836} {:answer/id #uuid "a816432d-4895-55c2-953a-1699f10ab4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258249} {:answer/id #uuid "c83059f8-61f6-50d7-9ebe-a77e51cd568a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258528} {:answer/id #uuid "f86cf986-18fe-5093-aa80-ac15fc51982d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7fca20-5bdf-5366-9789-fd9cfe5d4ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258960} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e700bf1-abf6-54f9-a024-9c52cdfe7394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0148bc8-aabc-5dbc-868a-3905d42c3ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-125751" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}-2x)\\,i+2xy\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,1)$ to $(1,3)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(1(3)^{2}-{1}^{2})-(0(1)^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fdf40bb8-48bd-542e-b098-e878dafa270b"}] :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424090}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447007} :activity/id #uuid "f05955d9-91c2-56df-a6bb-8701f2348c16" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259854 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04edc601-2930-52d5-862b-aa70c1a53004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256824} {:answer/id #uuid "123bf47b-36cc-59d7-9d17-a22088107f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256948} {:answer/id #uuid "7d949795-b889-5e52-9f41-bc81ed8d37b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257892} {:answer/id #uuid "88dd0768-8c63-597d-8960-8bce308cab84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257983} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a0d9dc-f41e-5996-a270-62b9910bf70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258622} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4bee5c-5730-5fd9-a144-30c0b35fffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8bad440-9dfc-5258-b308-3c0a302cd41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-125739" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,2,0)$ to $(-2,3,1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,2,0)$ and $B(-2,3,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-2,3,1)-f(-1,2,0) \\\\  & =(3(1)-(-2))-(2(0)-(-1)) \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22c4665d-8155-5ae8-8092-57bb12f99f4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b012eb-6902-5171-a714-6e9adcc3fdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257145} {:answer/id #uuid "729cf2e2-8525-5159-a943-e12c5d56a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257791} {:answer/id #uuid "744ef8ea-157c-58d8-8279-46f596d5d3ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257800} {:answer/id #uuid "a79e3f8e-8a0c-50b2-a4c1-c3b8e88aed24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} {:answer/id #uuid "d7dba790-e01b-5073-82cd-206cb42c4124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258244} :answer-field/id #uuid "82163bfb-0b92-5277-b853-9c511755cd36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "536580f0-4841-5ab4-989a-c614cdea00c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-125737" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+z.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,3)$ to $(0,-1,2)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(1,0,3)$ and $B(0,-1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(0,-1,2)-f(1,0,3) \\\\  & =({0}^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2)-({1}^{3}-{0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =1-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "293bc725-9204-5423-bbd2-c85d97ea1144" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2f20f0-48bb-505a-a0bb-3e4578a2d1ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446999} {:answer/id #uuid "888ab4fd-bbc3-5419-bfeb-a182fae50906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447000} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a59df4-b60e-57d1-8bb8-0ef8a574c18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ea3063-2fe4-5c0e-9794-3156030da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447002} {:answer/id #uuid "da2b77e0-70de-507a-844b-baad5473a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447001} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee6eda1f-7c7b-5e84-ad86-9a336560bf16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ccf7d0b-49a0-5735-9f73-94b4693e10fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-125740" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+{z}^{3}.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,-1,1)$ to $(2,1,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,-1,1)$ and $B(2,1,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(2,1,0)-f(0,-1,1) \\\\  & =(2(1)^{2}+{0}^{3})-(0(-1)^{2}+{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b5208760-54e7-5464-b42f-550fe3cbde8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10794cd8-12ac-5cb8-b8c1-b910715a1350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256933} {:answer/id #uuid "2a489fae-a9d9-5967-9682-2c7dc62d72b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257155} {:answer/id #uuid "c6210f7f-4a94-5eae-829e-7c2ebbb2ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258514} {:answer/id #uuid "f07868a6-417c-5ea4-8b12-1af3e2b84607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258874} {:answer/id #uuid "f623dcd4-0339-5e15-b9d6-9476f7a218e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258932} :answer-field/id #uuid "28961929-f635-5865-9f98-b89b6ca896ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "088dda9c-46fc-5710-8c3d-c31a5fce7d54" :question/math-academy-id "q-125755" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=12{x}^{3}\\,i-2\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,3)$ to $(0,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,3)$ and $B(0,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=3{x}^{4}-2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(0,-1)-f(-1,3) \\\\  & =(3(0)^{4}-2(-1))-(3(-1)^{4}-2(3)) \\\\  & =2-(3-6) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b83ca3f6-3386-5f07-be1b-40eb7acf84d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447010}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447010 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172303 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a30b11a-120a-5f7a-8652-df80e513b7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256875} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3dcd67-78eb-583f-97c5-32ac61d09e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258173} {:answer/id #uuid "adfaf3dc-e92b-57ed-a5f8-0dc1710ebeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258300} {:answer/id #uuid "af03ba57-28b4-5704-b56c-cd9a3133f7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1a8fbb-7b74-5316-bf06-61f28d3e5539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258307} :answer-field/id #uuid "075c178f-e1b3-5fc0-885f-31705c3ec578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "25a22695-171f-55bf-b6a5-1f3b602faf0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125760" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "da6e3be4-bbf8-5d1f-a071-2461ecbb29f8"}] :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446998}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550084} :activity/id #uuid "12f92483-dd03-5c8e-80ad-913e58e9d13e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150 :topic/id #uuid "deab20e7-248d-57af-ad42-5509f60f2d31" :topic/math-academy-id 3360} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a6ae28-ab05-53de-9b3f-5e347ef15023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415194} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4d724-583b-5a37-a444-101bb4a57ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} {:answer/id #uuid "32debd00-39de-5d6e-9e96-d38fb37c9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415208} {:answer/id #uuid "485effef-da1a-51e4-aa09-f8d8650ea3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415214} {:answer/id #uuid "d067cbee-9b2c-5c36-8dc8-c47522a4af35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ad03362-7885-5567-9b35-4c8299722802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3c651ce1-2ea6-5480-b7a8-389903a354b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125750" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(\\sqrt{3},2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{3},2)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-(1)^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "54271603-a8d8-52fd-b7c6-f038fe58c7c9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550068}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fc24743-a02f-5bf0-9839-ac80348908a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448232} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e9b1d4-bf42-5627-ad17-aca33e0e39b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448233}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448232} :answer-field/id #uuid "584fe0e6-b857-54a2-9215-9de39aae4c49" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449156} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc0dcb42-65e9-55a9-904d-f11371368f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448234} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd98ec7-8666-52b5-b988-4cbc4f87a080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448235} :answer-field/id #uuid "2031bd8d-6dd0-50f1-a070-e8d5e67ed206" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449157} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd954a98-2cf9-5746-8938-2c98f9e007e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448236} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3d7e0e-e48d-5344-a89f-52e9913c3871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448236} :answer-field/id #uuid "27965924-fed2-57f3-875b-59e349ddc1fc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449158} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84969817-3dcd-5fdb-8d9f-412b153421cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448238} {:answer/id #uuid "16347b9d-3533-5b71-8f9e-64d4326612b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448238} :answer-field/id #uuid "065de688-3909-5264-8ef5-ac4a05f8d35e" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fb181d0-594b-53e9-a69e-a9a120629b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125791" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,{x}^{2}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{5}{6},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{3{t}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."} :step/id #uuid "72ddc09a-f634-56d2-8672-530624c19756" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0625084b-e9ca-5f9d-9edb-7cd203c49e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256835} {:answer/id #uuid "2332677c-db38-5732-86a6-edbc38915c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} {:answer/id #uuid "767cc64e-5a0a-54f8-b56e-81ea5d23171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257822} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcf9ffb-d8c0-51f0-a661-b433dc2b60e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257878} {:answer/id #uuid "e0403918-536a-524a-8c14-ab0744be6702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076} :answer-field/id #uuid "83b90cfc-e328-5b69-bfa9-ca822accdfd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "157b91e2-9d0a-5ac1-ac9e-d1e250dcce18" :question/math-academy-id "q-125748" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2xy{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,-1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(1,-1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y{e}^{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,-1)-f(-1,1) \\\\  & =(-1){e}^{{1}^{2}}-(1){e}^{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-e-e \\\\  & =-2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "aaa71730-f034-5e00-a0b4-12af9bb50f16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550055}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc80e04-300d-575e-befb-8d927f5a1393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463542} {:answer/id #uuid "11a2cad8-574b-5542-b2bb-a30e735d1d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463543} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6de86d3-8240-540a-9b53-90a7214df88d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464705} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dc0b456-6310-5ad5-b10c-eb2af4c2fc70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463544} {:answer/id #uuid "84cbd523-8193-5a7a-b8cd-96a8abf8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463545}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463544} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1caf6b-e6da-5368-a8d4-722507ce7684" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be16c156-3162-5023-ad80-da11f2f6bcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463546} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8d6bcb-9ee4-5850-8817-08b222a8bfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463546} :answer-field/id #uuid "50cc552f-f6da-53d0-8115-14a52ecbc6c5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af2b663-f664-5687-8b3d-4cb7ed3e89ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463548} {:answer/id #uuid "12a3b8bc-c982-5ad8-998b-0a6fb0036f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463548} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b4e1b4-58bf-5e0a-bc9c-2b1223fdcc3c" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "528091bc-3b41-5908-b5b6-b20261706098" :question/math-academy-id "q-125792" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨1,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(1,0)$ and $B(e,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2(e-1),$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,e].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}⟨1,\\,t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}1+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t(e-1)+1,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨1,\\,t(e-1)+1⟩\\cdot ⟨e-1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}e-1+t(e-1)+1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t(e-1)+e\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}(e-1)}{2}+et]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-1}{2}+e \\\\  & =\\frac{3e-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."} :step/id #uuid "cf11a650-818b-5113-814d-d314e6ffeae5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbc76e1-b401-5a78-abad-35dac56a605e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453342} {:answer/id #uuid "279f518b-43b4-54ff-a27b-f222f36cb0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453343} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ab559b0-8ad8-5600-af4d-58237670463b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2848ae05-afe6-565a-9e41-aea348ad8825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453344} {:answer/id #uuid "b066d196-4087-59b9-80a0-674602ceaaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453345} :answer-field/id #uuid "029fd954-167e-57fe-8991-0827db39c02c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454422} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9edb55-47b4-5ffa-8f84-e71046dcce7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} {:answer/id #uuid "9b304306-6e7d-5cf9-b636-c3306a1cc7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb5c926-ae08-5e20-a8c6-c74a02337973" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2382595d-1329-5487-addb-48a96fc302ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaebe90-5c00-5752-8300-47cb5c96cc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453348} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ae2286-b965-58b3-9eee-07c2cde691c4" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b47df0de-b5ee-5562-b36a-a659cbe48d85" :question/math-academy-id "q-125796" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,x⟩$ and the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."} :step/id #uuid "f0952fec-ef63-5ff2-9799-e1132e4419d4"}] :activity/title "Path Independence of Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549301}]}
